        "id": 1884045
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-09-28T06:42:21Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I got your point. It\u0027s better to fail the build rather than allowing it to continue with problems silently. I filed b/201371822 to track this.\n\nThis should be easier to implement than a boot test and should catch most of the problems that a boot test would catch.\n\nThe benefit of having the build-time test when compared to the boot test is that it can be easily controlled. For example, it can be controlled by the same flag that dexpreopting uses, so it won\u0027t fail the build if dexpreopting is turned off for a device.",
      "parentUuid": "40cdee15_7a769909",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 188,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 192,
        "endChar": 5
      },
      "revId": "204356f3b54be5936e152815a96d1e917022d077",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "6627c434_fb376e42",
        "filename": "apex/prebuilt.go",
        "patchSetId": 10
      },
      "lineNbr": 192,
      "author": {
        "id": 1373864
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-09-28T11:02:49Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "As we discussed in the standup, I think there\u0027s good reason for both:\n\nAn error at build is useful to give a clear early signal if we fail to build the dexpreopted files when the product is configured to use them. The  build_test\u003cn\u003e CI builds essentially does `m nothing` (i.e. Soong, Kati, and Ninja to check the build graph) on every available product, so any error detected at those stages we get covered even if there isn\u0027t a build that creates an image and flashes it to a test device.\n\nA test at boot is still useful to ensure that the dexpreopted files are successfully used by the runtime. Ideally we should have a test that gets activated automatically for all devices that are configured to do dexpreopting; if there are /system/framework/oat files then they also are mapped by the zygote and system server. I think you said that the odrefresh end-to-end test does this, so maybe it\u0027s just a matter of adapting it to work for the OAT files in the system image. Or perhaps such a test already exists? This is after all something that was already in place before the modularization effort started.",
      "parentUuid": "7dc4a5de_06d3436c",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 188,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 192,
        "endChar": 5
      },
      "revId": "204356f3b54be5936e152815a96d1e917022d077",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "4e9e3d33_6aaf6991",
        "filename": "apex/prebuilt.go",
        "patchSetId": 10
      },
      "lineNbr": 192,
      "author": {
        "id": 1884045
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-09-29T06:48:45Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Makes sense to me. There can be cases where the dexpreopted files exist but cannot be used (because of checksum mismatch, for example). I can see the necessity of having a boot test. We have a similar test in odsign_e2e_tests (https://cs.android.com/android/_/android/platform/art/+/2007f8591ca475abfbd1026f72bf8618b562e0f6:test/odsign/test-src/com/android/tests/odsign/OnDeviceSigningHostTest.java;l\u003d80;drc\u003d59064ebccc2e4b78d8f0dfa5e058c3d4de1fb36f), so it isn\u0027t hard to add another one. Thank you for the suggestion. I created b/201491591.\n\nOne thing we should consider is that the test may trigger a false alarm on devices with dexpreopting disabled on purpose. Therefore, we should do checks only if /system/framework/oat contains dexpreopted files, so that we won\u0027t get into trouble. This essentially makes the build test and the boot test orthogonal: the build test verifies that the dexpreopted files are generated, while the boot test verifies that they are used if they are generated.",
      "parentUuid": "6627c434_fb376e42",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 188,
        "startChar": 0,
